{"_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": ""}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "Interpreter: Concept and Use"}, {"type": "position", "content": 3}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 3}, {"type": "level", "content": "A1.2"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you use when you talk about interpreters. "}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Describe common simple incidents at work in a simple spoken or written situation with a supportive interlocutor. Represent the solution outline to the problem presented by the customer through making case of use diagrams, facing the problem to the customer following rules and protocols of the organization."}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Communicates effectively in English about the communicative topics for this level through the use of appropriate basic vocabulary and grammatical structures, and with intelligible pronunciation."}, {"type": "secondary_2", "content": "Exchanges simple information on programming tools and solutions to a client's requirement."}], "type": "objectives"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "enpe8n2le03ob03re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "There is / There are in Affirmative and Interrogative Forms"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "In order to express the idea of the existence of something both in singular and plural forms, the English language combines the word <span class=\"strong\"> there </span> and the verb <span class=\"strong\"> to be </span>."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Is there any translation process?"}, {"type": "text", "content": "You use <span class=\"strong\"> there is </span> to refer to singular or uncountable nouns, and <span class=\"strong\"> there are </span> to refer to plural nouns. <br/><br/>  Examples: <br/> <span class=\"italic\"><span class=\"strong\"> There is </span> an instant interpretation of source code instructions. <br/> <span class=\"strong\"> There are </span> different kinds of interpreters.   </span> <br/><br/> We use the interrogative form to ask for <span class=\"strong\"> details in the information </span>. <br/><br/> Examples: <br/> <span class=\"italic\"><span class=\"strong\"> Is there </span> an instant interpretation of source code instructions? <br/> <span class=\"strong\"> Are there </span> different kind of interpreters?  </span>  <br/><br/> When making interrogative sentences, you use <span class=\"strong\"> any </span> when you want to inquire about objects in plural or when you are mentioning uncountable nouns. <br/><br/>  Examples: <br/> <span class=\"italic\"><span class=\"strong\"> Is there any </span> interpreter for precompiled code? <br/> <span class=\"strong\"> Are there any </span> variations of interpreters? </span>  <div class=\"center_text\"> </br></br> <span class=\"strong\"> AFFIRMATIVE FORM</span> </div>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Are there any specific applications referring to interpreters?"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Well, there is a bunch of interpreters I know about.  We have LISP and Darmouth Basic among those interpreters that do parsing, and Phyton and Ruby which can work for precompiled data. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Interpreters don't always translate. In some cases, they parse the code and they can set its behavior instantly or sometimes an interpreter can also run precompiled codes.  "}, {"type": "table", "content": "{\"rows\":4,\"columns\":1,\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<span class=\\\"strong\\\">There is - present singular form</span>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"There is + noun (singular)+ complement\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<span class=\\\"strong\\\">There are - present plural form</span>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"There are + noun (plural)+complement\\\"}\"],\"styles\":\" intro_table_border\"}"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<span class=\"strong\"><div class=\"center_text\"> INTERROGATIVE FORM</div></span>"}, {"type": "table", "content": "{\"rows\":4,\"columns\":1,\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<span class=\\\"strong\\\">Is there - present singular form</span>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\" Is there + noun (singular) + complement? <br/> Is there any+ noun (singular) + complement?\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<span class=\\\"strong\\\">Are there - present plural form</span>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Are there + noun (plural) +complement? <br/> Are there any + noun (plural) +complement?\\\"}\"],\"styles\":\" intro_table_border\"}"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Specific Information About Variations of Types of Interpreters"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "<p><strong>Interpreters </strong>can run source code without a previous compilation process.</p>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Depending on their functional features, there are different types of interpreters: "}, {"type": "table", "content": "{\"rows\":1,\"columns\":1,\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<p style=\\\"text-align: center;\\\"><strong>Variations of Types of Interpreters</strong></p>\\\"}\"],\"styles\":\" intro_table_border instruction_table_background\"}"}, {"type": "table", "content": "{\"rows\":5,\"columns\":2,\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Bytecode Interpreters\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"These interpreters translate source code into an intermediate language called bytecode. We can run bytecodes in different computer systems and they are processed in virtual machines. One drawback of this interpreter is that they can't keep a global connection between the statements. Emacs Lisp is an example of bytecode. \\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Threaded Code interpreters\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"They generate pointers instead of bytecodes.  Each word of a pointer contains an instruction connected to a specific task or sequences of tasks.  A threated code interpreter processes instruction by instruction. An example of this type of code is Forth. \\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Abstract Syntax Tree interpreters\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"AST are faster than other interpreters since they parse each statement just once and they are able to keep the global structure of a source code. However, AST can sometimes lead to overloading. \\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Just-in-time compilation\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"JIT compilation allows to transform an intermediate representation of a code to a computer language at the same time of a program's execution. Some examples are Javascript and NET framework.  \\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Self-interpreter\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"This kind of program possess a computer language that can interpret itself.  This means that the interpreter needs to implement the same language in its system as the expected in the output code.  Basic can be an example of this type of interpreter.  \\\"}\"],\"styles\":\" intro_table_border\"}"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "intermediate language"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a representation of source code after an interpretation or compilation process"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Some interpreters are able to translate a source code language into an intermediate language."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n2le03ob03aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "parse "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to make an analysis of the parts of an structure "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: In some cases, they parse the code and they can set its behavior instantly."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n2le03ob03aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "bunch "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "refers to a group of objects or people  "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Well, there is a bunch of interpreters I know about."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n2le03ob03aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "glue language"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a type of scripting language "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: First, interpreters can run command languages or glue languages. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n2le03ob03aud05"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "sandboxing "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "referring to a security process that separates a program from other in case of representing a risk for other programs.  "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Third, they allow virtualization and lastly, they apply relevant security standards with sandboxing technology."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe8n2le03ob03aud06"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Imagine you are a programmer."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Choose a variation of the types of interpreters.  "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Look for information about the one you chose."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Prepare an oral presentation about it for your classmates."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Present your job to your class.   "}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "I recognize variations in the types of interpreters."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I understand the concept of interpreter. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "I explain the applications for interpreters."}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}], "type": "sena", "labs": [{"exercises": [{"stimuli_ids": [0], "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can say what an interpreter is. "}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": " I can list variations of different types of interpreters.  "}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can talk about applications for interpreters."}, {"type": "Free Header Item", "content": "Why do you think programmers need to translate source codes?"}, {"type": "Free Header Item", "content": "Do you think a compilation process is always necessary when programming? Why?"}], "type": "instructions"}, {"contents": [{"type": "group_name", "content": "Welcome"}], "type": "group"}, {"type": "feedback", "content": {"attempt_feedback": [{"type": "text", "content": "You have 2 tries"}, {"type": "text", "content": "you have 1 attempt left"}], "correct_feedback": "Correct", "incorrect_feedback": [{"type": "text", "content": "try again"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Maybe next time"}], "allowed_attempts": 2}}, {"type": "free_header_text", "content": "Ask your partner:"}, {"type": "free_type_text_area", "content": "Are there any other ways to translate source code?"}], "questions": [], "type": "WLC", "template_override": 1, "id": 0, "img_path": ""}, {"stimuli_ids": [2], "template_override": 1, "questions": [{"_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": ""}], "type": "question"}], "responses": [{"_data": [{"type": "text", "content": ""}], "selected": 0, "correct": 0, "id": 0}, {"_data": [{"type": "text", "content": ""}], "selected": 0, "correct":