incorrect_feedback": [{"type": "text", "content": "Whoops! Something important is missing."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Maybe next time..."}], "allowed_attempts": 2}}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Instructions: Complete the following steps. Fill in the Self-Assessment."}], "type": "post_instructions"}, {"contents": [{"type": "skill", "content": "Reading"}, {"type": "skill", "content": "Writing"}, {"type": "skill", "content": "Speaking"}], "type": "learning_skills"}], "id": 11}], "stimuli": [{"_data": [], "id": 0, "contents": [{}], "type": "welcome", "template_override": 1}, {"template_override": 1, "contents": [{"value": {"audiosrc": "enpe8n2le03ob03re2aud01", "images": [{"time": "0", "image": "enpe8n2le03ob03re2img01.jpg", "id": 0, "text": "<strong>Radio host:</strong>Hello, everyone, and welcome once more to our radio program, \u201cMachine language\u201d. Today, our guest is professor Miller. He is going to solve all our doubts about interpreters. Good morning, professor, and thanks for accepting our invitation. <br/>\n<strong>Professor Miller: </strong> Thanks for having me.  It's a pleasure to be here with the college audience. <br/>\n<strong>Radio host:</strong>Ok, professor, to begin with\u2026 what is an interpreter?"}, {"time": "28000", "image": "enpe8n2le03ob03re2img02.jpg", "id": 1, "text": "<strong>Professor Miller: </strong>  An interpreter is a program that can run encoded data without going through a compilation process. <br/>\n<strong>Radio host:</strong> How is that possible? Is there any translation process? <br/>\n<strong>Professor Miller: </strong>  Yes, some interpreters are able to translate a source code language into an intermediate language and they can execute instructions immediately like LISP and Darmouth Basic. <br/>\n<strong>Radio host:</strong> I see, is it always like that? Or are there any other features?<br/>\n<strong>Professor Miller: </strong>   Interpreters don't always translate. In some cases, they parse the code and they can set its behavior instantly or sometimes an interpreter can also run precompiled codes.  <br/>\n<strong>Radio host:</strong> Ok. Do you know of interpreters that can be examples of this? <br/>\n<strong>Professor Miller: </strong>   Well, there is a bunch of interpreters I know about.  We have LISP and Darmouth Basic among those interpreters that do parsing, and Phyton and Ruby which can work for precompiled data. There are some programs like Smalltalk that can create intermediate representations and execute precompiled information at the same time."}, {"time": "104000", "image": "enpe8n2le03ob03re2img03.jpg", "id": 2, "text": "<strong>Radio host:</strong>Are there any specific applications for interpreters? <br/>\n<strong>Professor Miller: </strong>  Yes, there are mainly four applications for them. First, interpreters can run command languages or glue languages.  Second, they make it easier to apply self-modifying codes. Third, they allow virtualization and lastly, they apply relevant security standards with sandboxing technology. <br/>\n<strong>Radio host:</strong>  I see exactly what you mean\u2026"}], "coverimg": "enpe8n2le03ob03re2img01.jpg"}, "type": "slideshow"}], "type": "audio", "_data": [], "id": 2, "title": ""}], "id": 0, "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "has_nav", "content": true}, {"type": "has_exercise_controls", "content": true}], "type": "lab_options"}], "type": "scholar"}]}